    <li class="toc-item" id="toc-s-objective"><a href="#s-objective"><span class="toc-num">04</span> Task-Completion + PrefixLM</a></li>
    <li class="toc-item" id="toc-s-ablation"><a href="#s-ablation"><span class="toc-num">05</span> Ablation</a></li>
    <li class="toc-item" id="toc-s-results"><a href="#s-results"><span class="toc-num">06</span> Results</a></li>
  </ol>
</nav>

<!-- MIDDLE: ARTICLE -->
<article class="post-body">

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════ §01 ═══ -->
<section id="s-problem" data-code="problem">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Compute Divide</div>
<h2>Pretraining is a Club Most Can't Afford to Join</h2>
<p>
  The dominant pretraining recipe — autoregressive training on internet-scale raw text — works, but it concentrates foundational AI research inside a handful of compute-rich organizations. A 7B-parameter model like OLMo3 consumes roughly 252× the training FLOPs of HRM-Text. Qwen3.5 2B uses 36 trillion training tokens — 900× more than 40 billion. For academic groups, small labs, or individual researchers, this is an insurmountable wall.
</p>
<p>
  HRM-Text asks: what if you co-designed the architecture and the objective together, specifically for the data- and compute-limited regime? Instead of brute-forcing a Transformer through as many tokens as possible, can smarter architecture + smarter training signal make each token worth far more?
</p>

<!-- Efficiency scatter: tokens vs benchmark score -->
<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">efficiency_scatter.py — tokens vs avg benchmark score</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">interactive — hover over dots</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="eff-canvas" style="width:100%;height:280px;display:block"></canvas>
    <div id="eff-tooltip" style="position:absolute;background:var(--card2);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:6px;padding:.4rem .7rem;font-size:.7rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--text2);pointer-events:none;display:none;z-index:10"></div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  The chart above places each model at its training token count (x-axis) and average benchmark score (y-axis). HRM-Text sits in the <em>same performance band</em> as models trained on 100–900× more tokens — but at the leftmost edge of the x-axis.
</p>
<p>
  Three ingredients make this possible: a hierarchical recurrent architecture that computes more per parameter, a normalization scheme that keeps deep recurrence stable, and a training objective that concentrates every gradient update on the signal that actually matters.
</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════ §02 ═══ -->
<section id="s-hrm" data-code="hrm">
<div class="section-no">02 — Dual-Timescale HRM</div>
<h2>Slow Strategy, Fast Execution</h2>
<p>
  Standard Transformers process a sequence in one forward pass — a single sweep through layers. The Hierarchical Recurrent Model (HRM) instead runs the same parameters <em>multiple times</em> in two nested loops, inspired by the frontoparietal loop in the brain: a fast layer for execution and a slow layer for strategic context.
</p>

<!-- Architecture SVG -->
<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">hrm_architecture.py — dual-timescale recurrent design</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">H = slow · strategic · global; L = fast · execution · local</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body" style="min-width:0;overflow-x:auto">
    <svg id="hrm-svg" viewBox="0 0 640 310" style="width:100%;min-width:500px;display:block;font-family:var(--ff-mono)">
      <!-- Background grid lines -->
      <defs>
        <marker id="arr" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" refX="6" refY="3.5" orient="auto">
          <polygon points="0 0,7 3.5,0 7" fill="rgba(255,255,255,0.35)"/>
        </marker>
        <marker id="arr-p" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" refX="6" refY="3.5" orient="auto">
          <polygon points="0 0,7 3.5,0 7" fill="var(--purple)"/>
        </marker>
        <marker id="arr-c" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" refX="6" refY="3.5" orient="auto">
          <polygon points="0 0,7 3.5,0 7" fill="var(--cyan)"/>
        </marker>
        <marker id="arr-g" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" refX="6" refY="3.5" orient="auto">
          <polygon points="0 0,7 3.5,0 7" fill="var(--green)"/>
        </marker>
      </defs>

      <!-- Input embed -->
      <rect x="20" y="125" width="80" height="36" rx="6" fill="rgba(6,182,212,.12)" stroke="rgba(6,182,212,.5)" stroke-width="1.2"/>
      <text x="60" y="138" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--cyan)" font-size="9" font-weight="600">Input</text>
