<p>
  HRM uses <strong>2 outer H-cycles</strong>, each containing <strong>3 fast L-module steps</strong> followed by <strong>1 slow H-module step</strong>. That's 8 total forward steps through the same recurrent core — equivalent to 4× the compute of a single-pass Transformer of the same size, without 4× the parameters. The L-module refines local execution (token-level attention and FFN), while the H-module integrates global strategic context across the entire sequence.
</p>
<p>
  This is the key efficiency insight: <em>depth through time, not through width</em>. Instead of stacking 32 unique layers, HRM reuses a compact core across recurrent steps, achieving more effective computation per parameter.
</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════ §03 ═══ -->
<section id="s-magicnorm" data-code="magicnorm">
<div class="section-no">03 — MagicNorm</div>
<h2>Making Deep Recurrence Trainable</h2>
<p>
  Deep recurrence amplifies a known problem in Transformer design: where you place normalization layers fundamentally changes the gradient dynamics. <strong>PostNorm</strong> (norm outside the residual) keeps activations bounded but blocks gradients in deep networks. <strong>PreNorm</strong> (norm inside the residual) lets gradients flow but allows residual variance to grow unboundedly — causing representation collapse.
</p>
<p>
  In a recurrent model this matters enormously: the <em>same transformation</em> is applied N times. PostNorm's vanishing gradient problem compounds multiplicatively. PreNorm's variance growth also compounds. Neither option survives deep recurrence at language-model scale.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">magicnorm.py — forward variance vs backward gradient health</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click a norm strategy to see the tradeoff</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="norm-tabs">
      <button class="norm-tab active" data-norm="postnorm" id="norm-postnorm">PostNorm</button>
      <button class="norm-tab" data-norm="prenorm" id="norm-prenorm">PreNorm</button>
      <button class="norm-tab" data-norm="magic" id="norm-magic">MagicNorm ✦</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="norm-canvas" style="width:100%;height:220px;display:block"></canvas>
    <div id="norm-desc" style="margin-top:.75rem;font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted2);line-height:1.65"></div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  MagicNorm exploits the asymmetry between forward and backward horizons under truncated backpropagation through time (TBPTT). Each recurrent module consists of L internal PreNorm blocks, but is <em>capped with a final normalization at its exit</em>:
</p>
<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">MagicNorm formula</div>
  <span class="math-var">z<sub>n</sub></span> = Norm(<span class="math-var">z<sub>n−1</sub></span> + Σ Sublayer<sub>l</sub>(Norm(·)))
  <div class="math-comment">  ↑ exit norm bounds variance on forward path (N applications)</div>
  <div class="math-comment">  ↑ inner PreNorm identity path preserves gradients (only K &lt; N applications in backward pass)</div>
</div>
<p>
  Forward pass: the exit norm is applied N times — one per recurrent step — bounding activation variance at each step like PostNorm. Backward pass with truncated gradients (K ≪ N): the error signal only passes through K exit norms, while flowing through L inner PreNorm identity connections — behaving like PreNorm for optimization stability.
</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════ §04 ═══ -->
<section id="s-objective" data-code="objective">
<div class="section-no">04 — Task-Completion + PrefixLM</div>
<h2>Every Gradient Should Count</h2>
<p>
  Standard pretraining computes loss over <em>every</em> token — including prompt tokens that the model will never need to generate at inference time. When you train on instruction-response pairs like <code>Q: What is the capital of France? A: Paris.</code>, the loss on "What is the capital of France?" trains the model to predict context it already has. This wastes compute.
</p>
<p>
  HRM-Text applies a <strong>task-completion objective</strong>: compute loss exclusively on the response tokens <em>x<sub>a</sub></em>, conditioned on the full instruction <em>x<sub>q</sub></em>. Since the instruction is known at inference time anyway, there's no information in learning to reconstruct it.
</p>

<!-- Attention map contrast -->
<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">prefixlm_attn.py — causal vs PrefixLM attention patterns</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">prompt tokens shown in amber · response tokens in white</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="attn-grid">
      <div class="attn-panel">
        <div class="attn-label"><span>Causal masking</span> — instruction tokens autoregressively predict each other</div>
        <canvas id="attn-causal" style="width:100%;height:180px;display:block"></canvas>
      </div>
      <div class="attn-panel">
        <div class="attn-label"><span class="good">PrefixLM masking</span> — instruction tokens attend to all instruction tokens bidirectionally</div>
        <canvas id="attn-prefix" style="width:100%;height:180px;display:block"></canvas>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p style="font-size:.75rem;color:var(--muted);margin:.5rem 0 0">Example: <code style="color:var(--amber)">What is the capital of France?</code> (4 tokens, amber) + <code style="color:var(--text2)">Paris</code> (1 token, white). In PrefixLM, all instruction tokens see each other bidirectionally; loss is only computed on the response.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  The PrefixLM attention mask pairs naturally with the task-completion objective. Since the model never predicts instruction tokens autoregressively, it doesn't need causal masking over them. Instead, all instruction tokens attend to each other bidirectionally — giving the model an encoder-like view of the full prompt before generating the response.
</p>
<p>
  The empirical payoff: attention entropy increases (the model distributes attention more broadly across the prompt), and response-token NLL decreases — more informative gradients per training token.
</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════ §05 ═══ -->
<section id="s-ablation" data-code="ablation">
<div class="section-no">05 — Ablation</div>
<h2>Three Ingredients, Each One Matters</h2>
<p>
  To isolate the contribution of each component, the paper runs an incremental ablation across all three axes: architecture (Transformer → HRM), objective (full causal → response-only), and attention (causal → PrefixLM). All experiments are FLOPs-matched.
</p>

